{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>And Elohim made the rakia.</b> Since, when some of the elemental waters departed from beneath that portion of them which had been transformed into vaporous nature, as He said: יִקָּווּ הַמַּיִם מִתַּחַת הַשָּׁמַיִם (let the waters be gathered under the heaven), it was fitting that the vaporous portion then descend to the place from which those waters had withdrawn. Instead, that rakia which separates was made to have the power to restrain and hold back the vaporous portion so that it would not descend — and this is the waters above the rakia — in order that the compressed airy portion descend and the vaporous portion remain in its original place. Therefore when the moist vapor arrives there it condenses and produces rain, snow, and hail, and when these condense they become heavy and fall, as it says: לְקוֹל תִּתּוֹ הֲמוֹן מַיִם בַּשָּׁמַיִם (Jeremiah 10:13). By הַשָּׁמַיִם he means the rakia that thickens the airy portion, as it says: וַיִּקְרָא אֱלֹהִים לָרָקִיעַ שָׁמָיִם (Genesis 1:8). And when the fiery vapor arrives there it produces thunder and lightning, as it says: מַעֲלֶה נְשִׂיאִים מִקְצֵה הָאָרֶץ בְּרָקִים לַמָּטָר עָשָׂה (Psalms 135:7). And since some of the heavy watery element is above the light air that is near us, contrary to their natures, this indicates the operation of an agent of will directing toward an end, without doubt, as it says: וּמַעֲשֵׂה יָדָיו מַגִּיד הָרָקִיעַ (Psalms 19:2)." "<b>And it was so.</b> It remained thus established, contrary to its nature."], :book :genesis, :voice :sforno, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T16:11:30.266529548Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}